#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ccapple -- read-only search of the Apple apps' local stores on this Mac:
Contacts, Messages, Calendar, Reminders, Notes, Photos and Home.

Why it exists: the data a person keeps in the Apple apps (the address book, the
calendar, reminders, notes, messages, the photo library) is reachable only
through the GUI apps or Apple's privacy-gated frameworks, neither of which a
Claude Code session can drive. The stores themselves are plain SQLite files that
iCloud already syncs onto the Mac, so a small reader needs no account, no new
API grant and no third-party tool -- only the Full Disk Access macOS already
makes the operator give a terminal before it will let that terminal open them
(System Settings > Privacy & Security > Full Disk Access). Without it `status`
reports each store as "CANNOT OPEN" and says why; nothing else fails obscurely.

THE FLOOR, IN PLAIN WORDS. These stores hold other people's messages and the
operator's private material, so:
  * READ-ONLY, BY CONSTRUCTION. Every store is opened with the SQLite URI
    `file:<path>?mode=ro&immutable=1` plus `PRAGMA query_only=ON`. `mode=ro`
    refuses writes; `immutable=1` also takes no lock and never touches the
    -wal/-shm files, so the app that owns the store is never disturbed. Nothing
    here writes, copies a store, or drives a GUI app (no osascript, no `open`).
  * SEARCH, NEVER DUMP. Every search subcommand refuses to run with no filter,
    and every search is capped by --limit (default 20). Whole-store extraction
    is not a feature.
  * `immutable=1` reads the main database file only and skips the uncommitted
    write-ahead log, so the newest few rows (the last few messages, an edit
    made seconds ago) can be missing. It can in principle also read a torn page
    if the app is mid-write. Fine for search; re-check a value that matters.
  * Sending a message, or writing to any Apple app, is not here. It would need
    the operator's explicit approval first, on a briefing of what would change.
  * What a search prints goes wherever the caller's output goes -- into a
    session transcript, for a Claude Code session. Filter narrowly, and keep
    the output out of any repo, document or message shared with other people.

Usage:
  ccapple status                       # per store: path, opens read-only?, row counts only
  ccapple contacts  [--name N] [--phone P] [--email E] [--org O]
  ccapple messages  [--person NAME|HANDLE] [--since D] [--until D] [--text T]
  ccapple calendar  [--since D] [--until D] [--calendar C] [--text T]
  ccapple reminders [--list L] [--text T] [--open-only]
  ccapple notes     [--title T] [--text T] [--folder F] [--since D] [--until D] [--id N]
                    [--include-deleted] [--full]
  ccapple photos    [--text T] [--since D] [--until D] [--near LAT,LON[,KM]] [--album A]
                    [--uuid U] [--include-deleted]
  ccapple home      (--homes | --rooms | --zones | --accessories | --scenes | --automations)
                    [--home H] [--room R] [--text T]
  common: --limit N (default 20)  --json  --db PATH (override the store; repeatable
  for contacts and reminders; for photos it is Photos.sqlite inside the .photoslibrary)
  messages also takes --contacts-db PATH.
  Dates are YYYY-MM-DD (or YYYYMMDD) in local time; --until is inclusive of that day.
  Runtime: Python 3.8 or later (exercised on 3.9 and 3.14), standard library only.
  Full manual: man ccapple.

HOW EACH STORE IS READ (observed on macOS 26, not assumed; the layouts are
Apple's private business and can change, so a reader must fail loudly, never
guess -- every decoder below returns None or raises rather than invent a value)
  Contacts   ~/Library/Application Support/AddressBook/Sources/*/AddressBook-v22.abcddb
             (every source; one is often empty). ZABCDRECORD + ZABCDPHONENUMBER +
             ZABCDEMAILADDRESS. Names match accent- and case-insensitively, so
             "maori" finds "Māori". Phones match on digits, never on spaces.
  Messages   ~/Library/Messages/chat.db. Dates are Apple-epoch NANOSECONDS on
             modern macOS and seconds on old rows; ts_to_datetime() detects
             which by magnitude. The body is usually NULL in `text` and held in
             `attributedBody`, a typedstream blob (NSAttributedString archive).
             decode_attributed_body() pulls the plain NSString out of it
             BEST-EFFORT: it finds the "NSString" class marker, then the '+'
             (0x2b) string tag, then Apple's variable-length length prefix (one
             byte, or 0x81/0x82/0x83 followed by a 2/4/8 byte little-endian
             length). It ignores attributes (links, mentions, formatting), and
             returns None rather than guess when the shape is not as expected.
             Because most bodies live in the blob, --text cannot be a SQL LIKE:
             rows are decoded in Python while scanning newest first.
             --person is resolved through Contacts to phone/email handles; a
             value containing '@' or mostly digits is used as a handle as-is.
             One-to-one chats plus that person's own messages in groups are
             returned; other people's messages in a group are not.
             Handles match on the last 9 digits of a phone number (so +44 20...
             and 020... agree) and on a lowercased email.
  Calendar   ~/Library/Group Containers/group.com.apple.calendar/Calendar.sqlitedb.
             CalendarItem (entity_type 2 = event) + Calendar + Location. Times
             are Apple-epoch seconds, UTC; all-day events are stored at UTC
             midnight and shown as plain dates. RECURRING events are not
             expanded by this tool: inside a date range they appear only for
             occurrences in Calendar's own OccurrenceCache (a rolling window),
             and with no date filter they appear once, as the series master,
             flagged "repeats".
  Reminders  ~/Library/Group Containers/group.com.apple.reminders/Container_v1/
             Stores/Data-*.sqlite. Several stores exist; usually only one holds
             reminders (the others have the tables but no rows).
             ZREMCDREMINDER joined to ZREMCDBASELIST (the list). Rows marked for
             deletion are skipped.

  Notes      ~/Library/Group Containers/group.com.apple.notes/NoteStore.sqlite.
             SCHEMA (read from sqlite_master and Z_PRIMARYKEY): one table,
             ZICCLOUDSYNCINGOBJECT, holds notes, folders and accounts, told
             apart by Z_ENT. The Z_ENT numbers are not stable between macOS
             versions, so they are looked up by name in Z_PRIMARYKEY ('ICNote',
             'ICFolder'), never hard-coded. For a note:
             ZTITLE1 = title, ZMODIFICATIONDATE1 = modified (Apple-epoch
             seconds), ZFOLDER = its folder row, ZNOTEDATA = its ZICNOTEDATA
             row, ZISPASSWORDPROTECTED = locked. For a folder: ZTITLE2 = name,
             ZPARENT = parent folder, ZFOLDERTYPE (0 normal, 1 'Recently
             Deleted', 2 and 3 smart folders, which have ZSMARTFOLDERQUERYJSON
             set and are not counted as folders). A note is TRASHED when its
             folder has ZFOLDERTYPE=1 (or ZMARKEDFORDELETION=1).
             BODY: ZICNOTEDATA.ZDATA is a gzip stream holding a protobuf
             message. Path to the text, as field numbers:
                 root . 2 (Document) . 3 (Note) . 2 (string, UTF-8)
             i.e. decode root, take its length-delimited field 2, inside that
             take field 3, inside that take field 2: the whole note as plain
             text, first line = the title. Siblings seen under field 3 are
             repeated field-3 attribute runs (formatting spans, not text);
             attachments appear in the text as U+FFFC, shown here as
             "[attachment]". Every other field is ignored. The reader
             (_proto_fields) is a minimal stdlib wire-format walker: varint (0),
             fixed64 (1), length-delimited (2), fixed32 (5); a group wire type
             or a truncated field raises ProtoError and the note decodes to
             None, which search reports as "body not decoded", never as a guess.
             The gunzip is size-capped (MAX_NOTE_BYTES) against a decompression
             bomb. No third-party protobuf library is used or wanted.
             LOCKED NOTES (ZISPASSWORDPROTECTED=1): their ZDATA is encrypted.
             This tool never reads it and never attempts to decrypt: a locked
             note is listed (title, folder, date) flagged `locked`, with no
             text, and a --text search cannot see inside it.
             Search is by title, text, folder, modified date or --id. Each needs
             a filter; the default output is a snippet around the match, not
             the note. --full prints one note's whole text and only when the
             search matches exactly one note (or --id names it).

  Photos     ~/Pictures/Photos Library.photoslibrary (Photos.sqlite in database/,
             the search index in database/search/psi.sqlite, files under
             originals/ and resources/derivatives/). IN PLACE: both databases
             are opened with open_ro() and NOTHING is copied (Photos.sqlite can
             be gigabytes); the Photos app, PhotoKit and osascript are never
             involved.
             SEARCH INDEX (psi.sqlite). `groups` holds one row per searchable
             string: `category` says what kind (1203 = text Photos recognised
             inside the image, one word per row; 1202 = captions/descriptions
             the user typed, whole phrases; many other categories are places,
             people, scenes and are NOT used here) and `normalized_string` is
             the folded form, TERMINATED BY A NUL BYTE (so '= word' never
             matches; a LIKE '%word%' does). `ga(groupid, assetid)` links a
             group's rowid to `assets`' rowid, and `assets.uuid_0/uuid_1` are
             the asset's UUID as two signed 64-bit integers: the UUID's 16
             bytes are struct.pack('<qq', uuid_0, uuid_1), little-endian, i.e.
             uuid.UUID(bytes=...) upper-cased equals ZASSET.ZUUID (verified
             against every asset in a large library; big-endian matched none).
             psi's own `creationDate` is not Apple-epoch seconds, so dates come
             from Photos.sqlite instead. --text is a substring match on every
             word given, ALL words required, so "x9000" also finds "x9000b".
             ASSETS (Photos.sqlite). ZASSET: ZUUID, ZDATECREATED (Apple-epoch
             seconds, UTC), ZLATITUDE/ZLONGITUDE (-180 means "no location"),
             ZTRASHEDSTATE (1 = Recently Deleted), ZDIRECTORY + ZFILENAME (the
             stored original, relative to originals/), and the camera-roll
             name the user sees (IMG_1234.HEIC) in
             ZADDITIONALASSETATTRIBUTES.ZORIGINALFILENAME (joined on ZASSET).
             "Has recognised text" = a ZCHARACTERRECOGNITIONATTRIBUTES row with
             ZCHARACTERRECOGNITIONDATA, reached through
             ZMEDIAANALYSISASSETATTRIBUTES.ZASSET. Albums: ZGENERICALBUM (entity
             'Album', ZTITLE) and the join table Z_<album>ASSETS with columns
             Z_<album>ALBUMS and Z_<asset>ASSETS; those numbers move between
             macOS versions, so they are read from Z_PRIMARYKEY by name.
             FILES. With optimised storage most originals live in iCloud, not
             on the Mac: originals/<ZDIRECTORY>/<ZFILENAME> exists for only a
             small share of assets, so "original on disk" is a real per-asset
             question. The viewable fallback is the LARGEST image among the
             UUID's derivatives, which sit in three places, each in a sub-folder
             named for the UUID's first hex digit (upper case):
               resources/derivatives/<D>/<UUID>_1_105_c.jpeg   (about 768x1024; the common one)
               resources/derivatives/<D>/<UUID>_1_102_o.jpeg   (a bigger render, some assets)
               resources/derivatives/masters/<D>/<UUID>_4_5005_c.jpeg   (a small master thumbnail)
               resources/derivatives/cvt/<D>/<UUID>/<UUID>_cvt_t*.jpeg   (video frames)
             .THM files (video stills) and non-images are ignored. Assets that
             live in a shared-library scope (ZDIRECTORY is "<n>/<UUID>") have
             no derivatives here and report no viewable file. Converting
             HEIC/HEIF for viewing is the caller's job (sips); paths only.
             TRASHED assets are skipped unless --include-deleted. Edits made in
             Photos (adjusted renders) are not followed: the original or the
             stock derivative is what is returned.
             --near LAT,LON[,KM] (KM defaults to 1) takes a bounding box in SQL
             and an exact great-circle distance in Python.

  Home       ~/Library/HomeKit/core.sqlite: the Core Data store homed (HomeKit's
             daemon) keeps. LAYOUT ONLY, AND READ-ONLY: this reads what is in
             the home (homes, rooms, zones, accessories, scenes, automations),
             never CONTROLS anything; turning a light on or running a scene is
             out of scope here and would need the operator's explicit approval
             (no homed, no Home app, no `shortcuts`, no osascript, no `open`).
             WHICH FILE. HomeKit holds several stores. core.sqlite is the one
             with the data (ZMKF* tables). core-cloudkit*.sqlite hold CloudKit
             sync metadata and ZMKFCK* mirror rows (a different, partial copy:
             ANSCK* tables are sync bookkeeping); core-local.sqlite is local
             bulletin/notification state, and datastore*.sqlite are the
             pre-2023 store. Only core.sqlite is read here.
             TABLES (entity per Z_PRIMARYKEY, table name stable):
               ZMKFHOME        ZNAME, ZOWNED (0 = a home shared with the user),
                               ZDEFAULTROOM (its unnamed default room)
               ZMKFROOM        ZNAME (blank on the default room), ZHOME
               ZMKFZONE        ZNAME, ZHOME. A zone groups rooms through the
                               many-to-many join table Z_<room>ZONES_ with
                               columns Z_<room>ROOMS_ and Z_<zone>ZONES_ (the
                               <room>/<zone> numbers are entity numbers: they
                               move between macOS versions, so the tool finds
                               the join table by those column names)
               ZMKFACCESSORY   one row per device; Z_ENT subclasses (HAP,
                               Media, AirPlay, AppleMedia) named in Z_PRIMARYKEY.
                               ZCONFIGUREDNAME (the user's name for it) else
                               ZPROVIDEDNAME; ZMANUFACTURER, ZMODEL,
                               ZSERIALNUMBER, ZFIRMWAREVERSION (text),
                               ZACCESSORYCATEGORY (the HAP category number:
                               1-23 are the published ones; other numbers are
                               shown as numbers),
                               ZROOM, ZHOME, ZHOSTACCESSORY (the bridge or hub
                               a bridged device sits behind)
               ZMKFSERVICE     ZACCESSORY, ZNAME / ZPROVIDEDNAME, ZSERVICETYPE
                               = the 16-byte HAP UUID, big-endian; the HAP ones
                               end in the HAP base 0000-1000-8000-
                               0026BB765291 and the first four bytes are the
                               short type (0x3E accessory information, 0x43
                               lightbulb ...). Only well-known types are
                               named here; any other is shown as 0xNNN
               ZMKFCHARACTERISTIC  types, formats, ranges: NO live values are
                               stored, so the store cannot say whether a light
                               is on. Not read.
               ZMKFACTIONSET   scenes: ZNAME, ZTYPE ('HMActionSetTypeHomeArrival',
                               ...Departure, Sleep, WakeUp are the built-in
                               ones, 'UserDefined' are the user's own, and
                               'TriggerOwned' is the hidden scene an automation
                               runs and is not listed as a scene), ZHOME,
                               ZLASTEXECUTIONDATE
               ZMKFTRIGGER     automations: ZNAME, ZACTIVE, ZHOME, ZFIREDATE,
                               ZMOSTRECENTFIREDATE; kind from the entity
                               (EventTrigger / TimerTrigger). Linked to its
                               scene by the join table Z_<actionset>TRIGGERS_
                               (columns Z_<actionset>ACTIONSETS_, Z_<trigger>
                               TRIGGERS_), found by column name as above.
               ZMKFACTION      WHAT A SCENE DOES (its actions) has had 0 rows in
                               every store observed, so scenes are listed by
                               name and type only. Not a tool gap: the data is
                               not there.
             DATES are Apple-epoch seconds (UTC) like the other stores.
             CREDENTIAL-SHAPED COLUMNS EXIST in the same tables and are NEVER
             SELECTed: every query names the columns it keeps (no `SELECT *`),
             and none of these is among them. ZMKFACCESSORY: ZPASSWORD,
             ZWIFIUNIQUEPRESHAREDKEY, ZPUBLICKEY, ZPAIRINGIDENTITY*,
             ZPAIRINGSTOREMOVE*, ZBROADCASTKEY, ZMATCRED* / ZPREEXISTINGMATCRED*
             (Matter credentials), ZMATTERPAIRINGS, ZACCESSORYSETUPHASH,
             ZDEVICEIRKDATA, ZSMARTBULLETINBOARDNOTIFICATIONDATA; ZMKFHOME:
             ZOWNERPUBLICKEY, ZNFCREADERKEY, ZMATCREDGRK, ZTHREADOPERATIONALDATASET,
             ZPRIMARYWIFISSID; ZMKFCHARACTERISTIC: ZAUTHORIZATIONDATA. A new
             column is never exposed by accident, because nothing is selected
             by wildcard. Serial numbers and names ARE shown (they are what a
             caller needs): the output is a security map of the home, so it is
             for the operator's own use, never for pasting into a repo or a
             document shared with others.
             STALENESS. core.sqlite is checkpointed only now and then (its
             newest row can be days older than its -wal file), and
             immutable=1 skips the -wal. When a non-empty -wal sits beside the
             store the CLI says so on stderr; a device added in the last few
             days can be missing.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 store unreadable, 2 usage (including "no filter given").
"""

